<%- include('layout-start.ejs', { title: 'Configuration — Accessories', basePath, activeNav: 'getting-started' }) %>
<div class="main-container">
    <div class="getting-started-layout">
        <%- include('sidebar-docs.ejs', { basePath, manualChapter: 2, configStep: 2 }) %>
        <div class="getting-started-layout-main">
            <div class="full-width-section">
                <h1>Accessories</h1>
                <p>Points, signals, crossings, and other layout items are configured on the <strong>Panels DCC website</strong>. Each accessory has a name, a type that looks like the real thing, and a DCC number for every state you want to throw.</p>
                <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="<%= panelsAppOrigin %>/accessories" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open accessories on the Panels DCC website</a></p>
                <%- include('doc-screenshot-placeholder.ejs', { src: '/images/accessory-control-annotated.png', alt: 'Accessories in PanelsDCC Control', caption: 'Once saved, the same accessories appear in Control with live (best-effort) state.' }) %>

                <h2>Step 1: Choose a type</h2>
                <p>Click <strong>Add New Accessory</strong>. You first pick a type card — search or filter by category if the grid is long.</p>
                <div class="doc-icon-row">
                    <div class="doc-icon-card">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/point-rh-up.svg" alt="Point or turnout"/>
                        <span>Point / Turnout</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="doc-icon-card">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/two-aspect-home.svg" alt="Colour-light signal"/>
                        <span>Colour signal</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="doc-icon-card">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/four-aspect.svg" alt="Four-aspect colour signal"/>
                        <span>Four aspect</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="doc-icon-card">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/home-gwr.svg" alt="Semaphore signal"/>
                        <span>Semaphore</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="doc-icon-card">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/open-closed.svg" alt="Open or closed accessory"/>
                        <span>Open / closed</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="doc-icon-card">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/level-crossing.svg" alt="Level crossing"/>
                        <span>Level crossing</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="doc-icon-card">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/custom-accessory.svg" alt="Custom accessory"/>
                        <span>Custom</span>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p>There is also a <strong>Turn Table</strong> type for accessories that use several DCC numbers. Custom is the catch-all for lights, motors, and anything without a dedicated picture.</p>
                <p>For points and signals, pick the subtype that matches the geometry or aspect count (for example Point (Right up), 2 Aspect Home, Home Signal (GWR)).</p>
                <%- include('doc-screenshot-placeholder.ejs', { label: 'Accessory type picker' }) %>

                <h2>Step 2: Name and picture</h2>
                <p>Give the accessory a short <strong>Name</strong> (required, up to 20 characters) — something you will recognise on a panel, such as “P1” or “Up home”.</p>
                <p>The type image is used by default. You can upload your own picture or <strong>Choose from types</strong> to pick a different icon.</p>

                <h2>Step 3: Options and DCC numbers</h2>
                <p>Each row is one state the accessory can take (normal / switched, danger / clear, open / closed, and so on). For every row, enter the <strong>DCC number</strong> your command station uses for that state. Some types also ask for a <strong>direction</strong>.</p>
                <p>Pre-built signals and points usually start with the right rows already; you fill in addresses. Custom and open/closed types let you <strong>Add Option</strong> if you need extra states.</p>
                <%- include('doc-screenshot-placeholder.ejs', { label: 'Accessory options table with DCC numbers' }) %>

                <h2>Step 4: Save and repeat</h2>
                <p>Click <strong>Create Accessory</strong>. Add the other points and signals you will place on a panel next. You can also create an accessory later from the panel editor if you realise something is missing — see <a href="<%= basePath %>/configuration/panels.html">Panels</a>.</p>

                <h2 id="macros">Step 5: Macros (optional)</h2>
                <p>A macro is a named combination of accessories — for example “set the yard road” or “clear for the main”. Macros appear at the top of the Control accessories list so you can fire several commands in one tap.</p>
                <p>This is <em>not</em> automatic route setting. Routes that follow trains and sensors will be documented in a later chapter.</p>
                <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="<%= panelsAppOrigin %>/accessories/macros" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open macros on the Panels DCC website</a></p>
                <ol>
                    <li>Click <strong>Create New Macro</strong>.</li>
                    <li>Give it a name and, if you like, a short description.</li>
                    <li>Select the accessories that belong in the group.</li>
                    <li>For each <strong>state combination</strong>, choose the position each accessory should take.</li>
                    <li>Save. <strong>Location</strong> is filled in later from which panels use those accessories — it may be blank until a panel exists.</li>
                </ol>
                <%- include('doc-screenshot-placeholder.ejs', { label: 'Create macro — accessories and state combinations' }) %>

                <h2 id="dcc-table">Advanced: Table view (DCC audit)</h2>
                <aside class="doc-tip-banner" role="note" aria-label="Skip if you only have a few accessories">
                    <p><strong>You can skip this</strong> until you have enough accessories that addresses might clash. The card view is enough for a small layout.</p>
                </aside>
                <p>On the accessory list, switch <strong>Card View</strong> to <strong>Table View</strong> (desktop). The table is organised by <strong>DCC number</strong>: one row per address, showing accessory name, type, panel(s), option, and direction.</p>
                <p>Use it to see which addresses are used by what, and to spot two accessories sharing a number by mistake. Click a name in the table to open that accessory.</p>
                <%- include('doc-screenshot-placeholder.ejs', { label: 'Accessory Table View — DCC numbers used by what' }) %>

                <%- include('getting-started-pager.ejs', {
                    pagerPrevHref: basePath + '/configuration/function-maps.html',
                    pagerPrevTitle: 'Functions and function maps',
                    pagerNextHref: basePath + '/configuration/panels.html',
                    pagerNextTitle: 'Panels'
                }) %>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<%- include('layout-end.ejs') %>
